just in the last several days i weighed  in on a tweet that in my view was  conveying some poorly thought out and  schmaltzy  and probably bad advice about how we  should think about ourselves in the  present and you've all seen this kind of  thing  in this case it was somebody talking  about how he was in politics and when he  got into it when he was 15 and we should  evaluate ourselves from the perspective  of our 15 year old selves and what they  would make of of what we're doing but  you've seen these kind of things all  over the place it could be 15 years old  it could be 13 years old it could be  eight years old it could be you know  your eighth grade self your kindergarten  stuff pick whatever you want it's  somebody from  your childhood or your adolescence who  is you  and you're supposed to  think about what they would think about  you or what they would say to you or  what they would make of you  in the present and of course this is  going to be  quite different depending on who we are  and how old we are in my case i'm 51  years old so if i pick my 11 year old  self that is a gap of 40  years and a lot has happened a lot has  changed not just in me but in the entire  world since that time and maybe you are  20 years old and you're getting this  advice about think about what your 11  year old self would say about you  now that you're out working and you've  got your own place or you're in college  or pick whatever else is going on you  know what would that that 11 year old  think about you what would they make of  you and i think that  in many respects  it could be helpful to to do something  like this but  it is really  poorly thought out advice and it taps  into  a kind of yearning and nostalgia for  a simpler time for  naivete for when you really had things  worked out and now there might be some  people who you know grew up with a great  home life and in in a great culture and  they were like proceeding along and  maturing the way that they should in  their you know childhood and teenage  years and and who knows how how long  we're going to go with is maybe into our  20s but that's not the case for a lot of  us  so just to cut to the chase again i'll  say this in that tweet i was like well  this is terrible advice i wouldn't take  any sort of direction or judgment or  verdict from that idiot 15 year old that  i was at the time  and i think a lot of people shouldn't  either i'm not even sure if i would take  it from my  25 year old self who's a bit closer to  me  in time and in life experience and you  know where that guy was  supposedly going with his life  so  there's a there's a number of uh  different things that i want to sort of  put on the table and this is kind of a  if the shoe fit wears it if this is  helpful for you that's great if not you  know it might just be kind of a  curiosity  so  the first thing i want to say is that  the disconnect is obviously going to be  greater depending on how far back you're  going and how old you currently are so  for people in my generation generation x  to go back to our childhood or  adolescence that is really  a big stretch and not just in terms of  our own age or what we've done or you  know what our health is like it society  has changed immensely  since then i mean i remember when i was  in high school a lot of us didn't even  think that we would finish high school  because we anticipated being in a hot  war with the soviets and warsaw pact and  that we would get you know drafted if if  the area wasn't you know destroyed by  nukes or something like that totally  different era isn't it compared to where  we are in the present or even where we  were  20 years ago so this disconnect  you know the how far it is  um how extensive it is it's really going  to depend on  who we are and  you know  i i would say that there's a lot of  things that stand in the way  of taking what my  earlier self whether it's you know  when i was eight whether i was 20 and  i'm going to give you i'm going to show  you some pictures of the guy who i used  to be in just a minute  what would separate me from him and make  him kind of a  not particularly helpful judge so it was  a different era  bringing  greg from 1987  or  1983 into the present  massive culture shock right i mean think  about the  mediation of our world through the  internet and mobile technology think  about the fact that you're probably  watching this if not on a phone  you're watching it on a tablet or a  laptop  or a desktop computer that has such  incredible  power and connectivity  that  you know what what we would have thought  of back then even the science fiction  isn't even close to where we currently  are think about  all the other things that have massively  changed  in the you know geopolitics of the world  or  what we know about the human body and  health or or the fact that you know  if it was 17 year old onward me he'd  probably be smoking cigarettes like  the proverbial chimney  and you know that's not something that  people are doing quite so much these  days so there's culture shock right and  then  at least in my case it wouldn't pay to  consult my 15 year old or 17 year old or  13 year old self  because that guy  was  very immature  and not just in terms of  being emotionally immature  that was a major problem he had  significant struggles with grief and  anger which there's still traces of  today but i'm a quite different person  he was you know quite insecure about a  number of matters there's also cognitive  and decision-making immaturity you know  our brains aren't fully developed and i  think there was a lot of you know  lack of information  at the time and we could talk about  experiential immaturity that guy  uh if we're going to look at somebody up  until i don't know maybe  well i think i started paying taxes when  i was maybe 17 or so although i was you  know earning back when i was 15.  but you know  there's holding down a job when you're a  teenager and then there's holding a job  as a long succession of jobs when you're  older  and uh you know i've had i've been a  house owner  i've got kids that i've i've had to you  know help raise and see through problems  of their own  uh i'm married with you know a great  spouse and all these sort of things i've  negotiated a whole bunch of health  challenges  um  you know  we could go on and on and on so that  that guy back then didn't know anything  about those those sort of matters except  what he saw adults going through  and he lived in a situation essentially  of dependency right he lived in a family  with a  house that he could go to or he was in  the army and the army was taking care of  him or  you know he went off to college and  lived in the the dorms you know school  uh structured life and told him what to  do from kindergarten on through uh 12th  grade he had jobs but they were kind of  menial jobs as well  and i think you know greg of the  1980s  and the 1990s maybe even into the 2000s  had all sorts of wrong-headed  assumptions about the way the world  worked and what was important and what  wasn't and a extensive lack of  information about many many things some  of which we probably are not all that  responsible for and some of which was  probably my own damn fault for not  paying attention or being interested in  the the right things  and um so i think it would be very  foolish  to make this person here i'll show you  these pictures you know so  these pictures are me  at various ages  from you know childhood  into my 20s when i was in college  this guy i mean look at him do you think  that he has his proverbial crap together  i don't looking back at it and i know  that he thought at the time  that he did  all these clothing choices all of these  ways in which he's looking he thought  that was cool back then or at least you  know um he was taking he was willing to  accept it the earlier on before i  started you know taking control over my  own um  garb and and 